{"id":"neet-2024-q002","pn":2,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Magnetism and Matter","st":"Magnetic susceptibility of materials","q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Material): A. Diamagnetic  B. Ferromagnetic  C. Paramagnetic  D. Non-magnetic\nList-II (Susceptibility χ): I. χ = 0  II. 0 > χ ≥ –1  III. χ >> 1  IV. 0 < χ < ε (a small positive number)\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV"],"c":2,"s":"Diamagnetic: χ is negative and between 0 and –1, so A-II.\nFerromagnetic: χ is very large, so B-III.\nParamagnetic: χ is small and positive, so C-IV.\nNon-magnetic: χ = 0, so D-I.","fig":{"w":360,"h":122,"alt":"Table matching materials A to D with susceptibility conditions I to IV.","d":[["r",5,5,350,112],["l",124,5,124,117],["l",5,37,355,37],["t",64,19,"List-I","m"],["t",64,33,"(Material)","m"],["t",240,20,"List-II","m"],["t",240,33,"(Susceptibility (χ))","m"],["l",33,37,33,117],["l",160,37,160,117],["t",9,51,"A."],["t",38,51,"Diamagnetic"],["t",128,51,"I."],["t",166,51,"χ = 0",""],["l",5,56,355,56],["t",9,70,"B."],["t",38,70,"Ferromagnetic"],["t",128,70,"II."],["t",166,70,"0 > χ ≥ –1",""],["l",5,75,355,75],["t",9,89,"C."],["t",38,89,"Paramagnetic"],["t",128,89,"III."],["t",166,89,"χ >> 1",""],["l",5,94,355,94],["t",9,108,"D."],["t",38,108,"Non-magnetic"],["t",128,108,"IV."],["t",166,108,"0 < χ < ε (a small positive number)","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q029","pn":29,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Magnetism and Matter","st":"Magnetic needle oscillation","q":"In a uniform magnetic field of 0.049 T, a magnetic needle performs 20 complete oscillations in 5 seconds as shown. The moment of inertia of the needle is 9.8 × 10⁻⁶ kg m². If the magnitude of magnetic moment of the needle is x × 10⁻⁵ Am²; then the value of 'x' is-","o":["50 π²","1280 π²","5 π²","128 π²"],"c":1,"s":"Time period T = 5/20 = 1/4 s, and T = 2π√(I/MB).\n1/4 = 2π√(9.8×10⁻⁶ / (M × 0.049)).\nSquare: 1/16 = 4π² × 9.8×10⁻⁶ / (M × 49×10⁻³).\nM = 4π² × 9.8×10⁻⁶ × 16 / (49×10⁻³) = 4π² × 9.8 × 16 × 10⁻³ / 49.\n= 12.8π² × 10⁻³ × 10⁻² × 10²\n= 1280π² × 10⁻⁵ Am².","fig":{"w":160,"h":100,"alt":"A magnetic needle with ends N and S hung by a thread from a support in a uniform field B shown by dashed horizontal arrows pointing right.","d":[["r",40,6,60,8],["l",44,14,49,6],["l",50,14,55,6],["l",56,14,61,6],["l",62,14,67,6],["l",68,14,73,6],["l",74,14,79,6],["l",80,14,85,6],["l",86,14,91,6],["l",92,14,97,6],["l",98,14,103,6],["l",8,40,128,40,"da"],["l",8,52,128,52,"da"],["l",8,64,128,64,"da"],["l",8,76,128,76,"da"],["l",66,14,66,62],["p","M44 82 L52 78 L88 44 L80 48 Z"],["t",88,34,"N"],["t",32,94,"S"],["t",134,68,"B"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q044","pn":44,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Magnetism and Matter","st":"Bar magnet, magnetic moment","q":"An iron bar of length L has magnetic moment M. It is bent at the middle of its length such that the two arms make an angle 60° with each other. The magnetic moment of this new magnet is:","o":["2 M","M/√3","M","M/2"],"c":3,"s":"Original moment: M = mℓ (pole strength m, length ℓ).\nEach arm has length ℓ/2 and the arms make 60°.\nThe distance between the poles is Δℓ = 2 × (ℓ/2) × sin30° = ℓ/2.\nNew moment M′ = m × ℓ/2 = M/2.","sfig":{"w":230,"h":295,"alt":"A straight bar of length l with M = ml, and the same bar bent at the middle into two arms of length l/2 making 60 degrees, the distance between the free ends being delta l.","d":[["l",5,18,198,18,"b"],["t",97,11,"ℓ","m"],["p","M5 33 L198 33 L198 52 L5 52 Z"],["t",9,77,"M = mℓ"],["p","M27 242 L220 242 L220 261 L27 261 Z"],["t",122,285,"ℓ/2","m"],["p","M30.6 242.7 L165.6 108.7 L154.4 97.3 L19.4 231.3 Z"],["l",15.1,227.1,150.1,93.1,"b"],["t",60,147,"ℓ/2","m"],["l",180,117,215,227,"b"],["t",205,175,"Δℓ"],["p","M52 242 A27 27 0 0 0 44 223"],["t",66,235,"60º"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q050","pn":50,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Magnetism and Matter","st":"Magnetic pole and sheet force","q":"A sheet is placed on a horizontal surface in front of a strong magnetic pole. A force is needed to:\nA. hold the sheet there if it is magnetic.\nB. hold the sheet there if it non–magnetic.\nC. move the sheet away from the pole with uniform velocity if it is conducting.\nD. move the sheet away from the pole with uniform velocity if it is both, non-conducting and non-polar.\nChoose the correct statement(s) from the options given below:","o":["A, C and D only","C only","B and D only","A and C only"],"c":3,"s":"A: a magnetic pole attracts or repels a magnetic sheet, so a force is needed to hold it. Correct.\nB: a non-magnetic sheet feels no force, so no force is needed to hold it. Wrong.\nC: if the sheet is conducting, eddy currents form in it and oppose the motion, so a force is needed to move it at uniform speed. Correct.\nD: a non-conducting, non-polar sheet does not interact with the magnetic field, so no force is needed. Wrong.\nSo A and C only."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q015","pn":15,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Magnetism and Matter","st":"Gauss's law for magnetism","q":"The net magnetic flux through any closed surface is :","o":["Negative","Zero","Positive","Infinity"],"c":1,"s":"For any closed surface, ∮B·dS = 0.\nMagnetic monopoles do not exist.\nSo the net magnetic flux through a closed surface is zero."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q106","pn":106,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Magnetism and Matter","st":"Susceptibility and permeability","q":"An iron rod of susceptibility 599 is subjected to a magnetising field of 1200 A m⁻¹. The permeability of the material of the rod is : (μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ T m A⁻¹)","o":["8.0 × 10⁻⁵ T m A⁻¹","2.4π × 10⁻⁵ T m A⁻¹","2.4π × 10⁻⁷ T m A⁻¹","2.4π × 10⁻⁴ T m A⁻¹"],"c":3,"s":"Relative permeability μᵣ = 1 + χ = 1 + 599 = 600.\nμ = μ₀μᵣ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 600.\n= 2.4π × 10⁻⁴ T m A⁻¹."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q099","pn":99,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Magnetism and Matter","st":"Earth's magnetism","q":"At a point A on the earth’s surface the angle of dip, δ = +25º. At a point B on the earth’s surface the angle of dip, δ = –25º. We can interpret that :","o":["A is located in the northern hemisphere and B is located in the southern hemisphere","A and B are both located in the southern hemisphere","A and B are both located in the northern hemisphere","A is located in the southern hemisphere and B is located in the northern hemisphere"],"c":0,"s":"A positive dip means the field points downward into the Earth, which happens in the northern hemisphere.\nA negative dip means the field points upward, which happens in the southern hemisphere.\nSo A is in the north and B in the south.","sfig":{"w":190,"h":160,"alt":"Earth's field resolved into BH pointing left and BV pointing down; the resultant points down-left and the dip angle δ is marked between BH and the resultant.","d":[["l",131,40,27,40,"a"],["l",131,40,131,140,"a"],["l",131,40,28,141,"a"],["l",27,40,27,140,"d"],["l",27,140,131,140,"d"],["p","M87 40 A44 44 0 0 0 100 71","a"],["t",72,64,"δ"],["t",22,28,"B"],["t",29,31,"H","s"],["t",136,150,"B"],["t",143,153,"V","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2018-q040","pn":40,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Magnetism and Matter","st":"Diamagnetism and energy","q":"A thin diamagnetic rod is placed vertically between the poles of an electromagnet. When the current in the electromagnetic is switched on, then the diamagnetic rod is pushed up, out of the horizontal magnetic field. Hence the rod gains gravitational potential energy. The work required to do this comes from","o":["the current source","the magnetic field","the lattice structure of the material of the rod","the induced electric field due to the changing magnetic field"],"c":0,"s":"A magnetic field itself does no work and the rod's lattice is not an energy source.\nThe energy stored in the electromagnet comes from the current source, which supplies the work for the gain in gravitational potential energy."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q166","pn":166,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Magnetism and Matter","st":"Apparent dip","q":"If θ1 and θ2 be the apparent angles of dip observed in two vertical planes at right angles to each other, then the true angle of dip θ is given by :","o":["tan² θ = tan² θ1 − tan² θ2","cot² θ = cot² θ1 + cot² θ2","tan² θ = tan² θ1 + tan² θ2","cot² θ = cot² θ1 − cot² θ2"],"c":1,"s":"If a vertical plane makes angle α with the magnetic meridian, the apparent dip θ′ satisfies tan θ′ = tan θ / cos α.\nPlane 1 at α: tan θ1 = tan θ / cos α, so cos α = tan θ / tan θ1.\nPlane 2 is at 90° − α: tan θ2 = tan θ / sin α, so sin α = tan θ / tan θ2.\nUse sin²α + cos²α = 1: tan²θ/tan²θ1 + tan²θ/tan²θ2 = 1.\nDividing by tan²θ gives cot²θ = cot²θ1 + cot²θ2.","sfig":{"w":400,"h":370,"alt":"Perspective sketch of two vertical planes at right angles; the magnetic meridian plane makes angle alpha with vertical plane (1).","d":[["l",151,155,291,128,""],["l",151,155,148,348,""],["l",148,348,291,310,""],["l",291,128,291,310,""],["l",255,8,291,128,""],["l",188,95,291,128,""],["l",255,8,255,270,"d"],["l",255,270,291,310,"d"],["l",188,95,188,295,"d"],["l",188,295,291,310,"d"],["l",130,260,143,246,"a"],["t",278,62,"vertical plane(2)",""],["t",91,110,"magnetic",""],["t",91,128,"meridian",""],["t",28,278,"vertical plane(1)",""],["t",218,131,"α",""],["p","M245 113 A48 48 0 0 0 244 137"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2016b-q028","pn":28,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 24 Jul","tp":"Magnetism and Matter","st":"Energy and torque of a bar magnet in a field","q":"A bar magnet is hung by a thin cotton thread in a uniform horizontal magnetic field and is in equilibrium state. The energy required to rotate it by 60° is W. Now the torque required to keep the magnet in this new position is","o":["W/√3","√3 W","√3 W/2","2W/√3"],"c":1,"s":"Work to rotate from 0° to 60° is W = MB(cos 0° − cos 60°) = MB(1 − 1/2) = MB/2.\nSo MB = 2W.\nTorque at 60° is τ = MB sin 60° = 2W × (√3/2).\nτ = √3 W."},
{"id":"neet-2016-q150","pn":150,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 1 May","tp":"Magnetism and Matter","st":"Susceptibility","q":"The magnetic susceptibility is negative for :","o":["paramagnetic material only","ferromagnetic material only","paramagnetic and ferromagnetic materials","diamagnetic material only"],"c":3,"s":"Paramagnetic and ferromagnetic materials have positive susceptibility.\nDiamagnetic materials are weakly repelled by a magnetic field, so their susceptibility is negative."}
